.docname {Wireless People Flow}
.include {docs}

Where people are in a building, live, from the Wi-Fi signals their phones already emit: a dot
per phone on the twin, and room counts stored as metrics.

**Status:** phase 1 built on simulated routers; not yet tried on a real OpenWrt site. Tracks
[issue #314](https://github.com/NickGhignatti/crowd-vision/issues/314).

**Applies to:** `devices/ap-collector`, telemetry (`/telemetry/observations`, three router
metrics), digital-twin (the estimator), socket (the `devices` event). No new service.

.box {Two phases, in order} type:{note}
    **Phase 1 — routers only.** Poll the access points the building already has, and listen to
    the probe requests they hear. Zero new hardware.

    **Phase 2 — dedicated sniffers.** Raspberry Pi rigs in monitor mode, for devices the
    routers miss. Only after phase 1 is proven in a real building.

---

## What Crosses Into CrowdVision

The Dutch DPA is explicit: *"Storing multiple location points per device means that the data
is not anonymous."* So the design keeps a phone's identity on site and never stores a position.

| Data | Where it exists | How long |
|---|---|---|
| MAC address | collector memory only | while the phone is heard |
| Token (random, `secrets.token_hex(8)`, never derived from the MAC) | signed tick, Kafka `wifi-observations`, twin memory | 10 min on Kafka; in twin, until unseen for 3 ticks |
| Position | twin memory (one per track, no history), Redis pub/sub `twin:devices:{id}` | the current tick only |
| Room counts | Timescale `readings` | the platform's retention (below) |

**Invariants:**

- **No MAC leaves the site.** The collector swaps every address for a minted token; nothing turns a token back.
- **No position is stored.** Twin keeps each track's current position only. Dots go out on Redis pub/sub, nowhere else.
- **A dot carries no id.** `twin_schema::Device` refuses unknown fields, so socket drops a dot carrying a token.
- **Only counts are stored**: `totalDeviceCount`, `ratioDeviceCount`, `estimatedOccupancy`.

.box {This is pseudonymous personal data while it is live} type:{warning}
    A token follows one phone for as long as it stays, and Kafka holds ten minutes of them. A
    dot has no id, but a viewer watching a near-empty building can follow one dot from tick to
    tick. So a site needs a legal basis and a notice before enabling it (Wi-Fi terms,
    employment agreement), exactly as for any Wi-Fi analytics. Turning off probes
    (`subscribeProbes: false`) limits it to people who joined the Wi-Fi.

---

## What Is Stored, And Why That Is Safe

A count carries no identifier and no per-device row, so there is nothing to single out. That
is the whole argument for the stored data, and it is only as strong as the two conditions below.

## Why Routers-Only Works

The obvious objection: MAC addresses are randomized, so won't one phone look like a different
person to every access point it passes?

No — **randomization is per-SSID, not per-AP.** Every AP in the building broadcasts the same
SSID, so a device roaming across them keeps one address.

| Platform | Behaviour on the building SSID |
|---|---|
| **Android 10–15** | Persistent randomized MAC derived from the network profile (SSID + security type). Stable across APs, stable across forget-and-rejoin, until factory reset. |
| **Apple** (iOS 18 / macOS Sequoia) | On WPA2-AES or WPA3, the private address is **fixed** per SSID. Only open and pre-WPA2 networks rotate — every 2 weeks. |

Wi-Fi roaming *requires* this. The DHCP lease, L2 forwarding tables and 802.11r fast
transition are all keyed on the station MAC. If it changed on roam, open TCP connections
would drop and roaming would not function.

.box {The objection is correct for the other regime} type:{warning}
    Probe-request MACs **are** random per scan burst and uncorrelated between listeners. So a
    probe address is never counted: the collector groups one address's frames into a burst
    (one scan), gives the burst a fresh token, and the twin only places it.

### Two regimes

| Regime | What the AP sees | Routers-only? |
|---|---|---|
| **Associated** — device joined the Wi-Fi | Every frame, continuously, stable per-SSID MAC, RSSI per AP | **Yes.** This is phase 1. |
| **Unassociated** — visitor never joins | Sporadic probe requests, streamed by hostapd (`subscribeProbes`, off by default) | **Partly.** Bursts place passing dots; counting them is phase 2. |

Phase 1 covers everyone on the Wi-Fi and misses the walk-through visitor. For facility
management the associated population is generally the population that matters.

It is also the only regime with a workable legal basis: a captive portal, Wi-Fi terms of
service or an employment agreement supports consent or legitimate interest. Ambient sniffing
of passers-by supports neither — that is what the AP fined a Dutch municipality for in 2021.
That is why `subscribeProbes` is off by default: a site turns it on only with a basis for it.

---

## Positions, Estimated in the Twin

Digital-twin owns the floor plan and the router placements, so it places the phones. Telemetry
never sees a coordinate, and no copy of the geometry can go stale when a router moves.

**How a phone is placed** (`service/estimator.rs`, `service/tracks.rs`):

- Each floor is cut into 0.5 m cells inside rooms. Each cell stores the signal every router on
  the floor should hear from it: −40 dBm at 1 m, path-loss exponent 2.2, 5 dB per wall.
- A connected phone's track is a posterior over cells: each tick weighs every cell by how well
  it explains the phone's signals, and by distance from the last position (spread grows
  0.5 m/s; cells beyond 2 m/s of walking are cut). A token unseen for 3 ticks is dropped.
- A burst (one scan heard by several routers) gets a fix: the cell whose expected signals fit
  best. It joins a track only when exactly one connected phone explains it within 9 dB. A
  burst fitting none is a passing dot (`connected: false`); one fitting two is dropped.

**Why a grid fit, not circles.** Fitting circles (trilateration) needs every distance right,
but walls and weak antennas only ever lower a signal, so every circle comes out too big.
Measured on 470 simulated bursts: right room 95% for the grid fit, 69% for circles.

**Measured on a recorded run** (`tests/data/wifi-recording.jsonl`, 15 min, 48 phones, simulated):

| | Dots | Each phone in its router's room |
|---|---|---|
| Right room | 79.4% | 75.0% |
| Room counts, total off | 1 462 | 1 988 |
| Position error | median 4.0 m, p90 8.1 m | — |

- Nearly all of it comes from memory between ticks, not from bursts: linking by place almost
  never fires, since most bursts fit two phones on one router.
- The simulator's radio and scan rates are guesses; a real walk test decides the real numbers.

**Fine Timing Measurement** (802.11mc/az/bk) stays rejected: it is client-initiated, so the
infrastructure never learns the position unless an app reports it back.

---

## Phase 1 — Routers-Only

.mermaid
    graph LR
        subgraph Building
            AP1[AP - lobby]
            AP2[AP - floor 2]
            AP3[AP - canteen]
        end
        C[ap-collector]
        AP1 -->|RSSI per station| C
        AP2 --> C
        AP3 --> C
        T2[GET /telemetry/collector] -->|routers: sensor id, room, ubus address| C
        C -->|Wi-Fi tick, tokens only, HMAC-signed| T[POST /telemetry/observations]
        T -->|router-room counts| PG[(Timescale)]
        T -->|tick as received| K1[[Kafka wifi-observations]]
        K1 --> TW[digital-twin estimator]
        TW -->|dots, no id| R{{Redis twin:devices:id}}
        R --> S[socket] -->|devices event| B[browser]
        TW -->|room counts| K2[[Kafka estimated-occupancy]]
        K2 --> T

### The collector

A small daemon on the building network. Polls each AP every `pollIntervalS` (10 s), holds one
in-memory token table, posts one signed Wi-Fi tick per building per poll to
`/telemetry/observations` (`schemas/fixtures/wifi-observations.json`). It counts nothing:
telemetry counts the tick per router room.

- **Which APs**: the router sensors placed in the twin, read from `GET /telemetry/collector` at start and every `syncIntervalS` (300 s). A router that stays keeps its session; telemetry unreachable keeps the previous list.
- **How to reach them**: the sensor's `endpoint` is the ubus URL (`endpointTemplate` fills a missing one); `driver` `openwrt-iwinfo` selects the iwinfo reader, anything else hostapd.
- **What it reports**: the routers that answered, every connected phone each one lists (roam leftovers dropped), and the probe bursts. Every tick is posted, even one where no router answered: an empty `answered` tells telemetry nothing was measured, so no room gets a fresh 0. Trimmed to the contract's limits (2000 rows, keeping the loudest; 2000 bursts, keeping the newest; 6 loudest routers per burst), since telemetry refuses an oversized tick whole. A refused or failed post is dropped, never retried: the next poll supersedes it.
- **How to log in**: the collector's own file — one site-wide `ubus` login and `ifaces`, per-router `overrides`. Never sent to the platform.
- **How it proves which building it is**: `keys: { "<buildingId>": "<device key>" }` in the same file, issued by an editor (`POST /telemetry/device-keys/buildings/{id}`). Each key reads that building's routers and signs its ticks; it cannot sign for another building.
- **Probes** (`subscribeProbes: true`): one hostapd `probe` stream per router interface, grouped each tick into **bursts** (`app/bursts.py`) — one address within 2 s of its first frame is one scan, keeping each router's loudest frame. A burst the tick cuts in half waits for the next tick. `--dry-run` prints counts, never an address or a token.
- **Tokens** (`app/tokens.py`): every address is swapped for a random token (`secrets.token_hex(8)`), minted, never derived, so nothing can turn one back. A connected phone keeps its token until it is missing for 3 ticks; a burst gets a fresh one, unless it was sent from a connected phone's own address. The `mac → token` table lives only in the collector's memory.

On OpenWrt the readings come from hostapd over `ubus` — `hostapd.<iface> get_clients` returns
every associated station with its `signal` in dBm. `iw dev <iface> station dump` is the
equivalent without ubus.

Per tick:

1. **Collect** `(mac, rssi, idle)` from every AP. iwinfo's `inactive` is the idle time. hostapd has none: idle is how long that station's `packets.rx` has not changed, so it is one poll coarse, and a station first seen counts as fresh.
2. **Drop roam leftovers.** A MAC listed by several APs keeps only its least-idle rows (within 1 s: each AP is polled on its own clock read). A station associates with one AP; the old one still lists it, signal frozen, until `ap_max_inactivity` (300 s).
3. **Tokenise**: swap every MAC for its token; group probes into bursts.
4. **Post** the tick. Telemetry then counts it: one token is one phone, counted once, in its loudest router's room.

.box {Union by phone, never sum per-AP client counts} type:{warning}
    Summing the client lists of overlapping APs is a straight multiplication of the count,
    and it produces exactly the symptom people expect from MAC randomization. One token on two
    rows is one phone; telemetry counts it once (`kernel/observations.rs`).

### Smoothing lives in the twin, transitions are not built

Telemetry keeps no state between ticks, so its router-room count can flip a phone between rooms
each tick. Digital-twin's tracks carry each phone across ticks, which is what smooths the dots
and `estimatedOccupancy`. Transition counts (`zoneTransition`, below) are still not built.

---

## The Platform Side

### Occupancy is three metrics, not one

Telemetry stores these for every room, every tick (the factor rides on the tick as `devicesPerPerson`):

| Metric | What it is | From |
|---|---|---|
| `totalDeviceCount` | Devices connected, each in its loudest router's room. A measurement. | telemetry, from the tick |
| `ratioDeviceCount` | Devices ÷ the site's devices-per-person factor. An estimate. | telemetry, from the tick |
| `estimatedOccupancy` | Connected devices placed in the room by position. An estimate. | digital-twin, Kafka `estimated-occupancy` |

`estimatedOccupancy` is never mixed into `totalDeviceCount`: the two place a phone differently,
and a dashboard can show both. It counts only rooms on a floor with a placed router, and only
ticks where a router answered.

Publishing only the estimate would be the smaller change and the wrong one. The factor is a
per-building guess that gets *re-measured*, and an estimate is only as good as
the factor it was derived with. Store the raw count and a corrected factor re-derives the
whole history; store the estimate alone and every past bucket is permanently wrong by whatever
the old factor was off by. The measurement is also the only one of the two that means the same
thing in every building, which is what makes cross-site comparison possible at all.

`ratioDeviceCount` is emitted only where the site actually configured a factor
(`useDevicesPerPerson`). No factor, no estimate — an estimate silently equal to the device
count is a claim about people that nobody made.

Both follow the shape of the existing `peopleCount` plugin
(`backend/telemetry/src/plugins/people_count.rs`): per building and room, non-negative
integer, dashboard catalog, twin overlay, agent tool.

**A room's count is its router's, not the room's.** A router hears devices wherever they
stand, across walls and floors, so the reading keyed to its room says "near this router".
The figure that means what it says is the building's: telemetry sums the rooms at the
building's newest report and serves it at `GET /telemetry/connected-devices/buildings/{id}`.

**One device, three metrics.** The access point is what gets registered as a sensor — device
kind `router` in telemetry's `plugins::devices()`, which reports all three. Registering it once
per metric would count one device three times.

**None carries a threshold.** A device count is an access-point capacity question, not a
facility one — it breaches on a room full of laptops. And the estimates beside it are guesses,
so a bound on them fires on the guess as much as on the building. Occupancy alerting stays with
`peopleCount`, which measures people instead of inferring them.

### Optional — per-AP client counts

Separate from occupancy, and useful for Wi-Fi capacity planning rather than for the twin:
how many stations each AP is carrying. It needs no deduplication, unlike the zone path — a
station is associated to exactly one AP at a time, so the per-AP lists cannot double-count the
way overlapping RSSI coverage can.

`readings` is keyed `(building_id, room_id, metric, ts)` with no AP dimension, and ingest does
not validate `room_id` against `building_rooms`. So the cheap shape is a distinct metric with
the AP name in `roomId`:

```
metric = "apClients", roomId = "ap-lobby-1", value = 14
```

Zero migration, existing index, existing hourly rollup. The alternative — the AP name as an
extra field in `payload` — stores fine but is not in the rollup's `group by`, so it never
aggregates, which is the entire point of collecting it. The cost of the cheap shape is that
these rows are only distinguishable from real rooms by their metric key; every query is
already metric-scoped, so it works, but it is a convention and not a constraint.

### Transitions (not built)

Counts of people moving from one room to another. Digital-twin's tracks now hold the data to
derive them; nothing emits them yet. One new plugin, following the same shape:

```rust
static DESCRIPTOR: MetricDescriptor = MetricDescriptor {
    value_field: "zoneTransition",
    key: "zoneTransition",
    label: "Zone Transition",
    interface_name: "IZoneTransition",
    unit: Some("people"),
    fields: &[
        FieldSpec { name: "buildingId",     kind: NonEmptyString,  required: true },
        FieldSpec { name: "roomId",         kind: NonEmptyString,  required: true },
        FieldSpec { name: "fromRoomId",     kind: NonEmptyString,  required: true },
        FieldSpec { name: "timestamp",      kind: Finite,          required: true },
        FieldSpec { name: "zoneTransition", kind: NonNegativeInt,  required: true },
    ],
};

static BOUNDS: &[BoundSpec] = &[BoundSpec { key: "maxTransitions", field: "zoneTransition", label: "Zone transitions", unit: None, direction: Above }];
```

`roomId` is the **destination**; `fromRoomId` is the extra field, so it lands in the stored
`payload` while the columns stay as they are — consistent with
[Telemetry Storage](telemetry-storage.qd). One registration line in
`backend/telemetry/src/main.rs` and the metric is live everywhere.

`maxTransitions` is not decoration: a sudden flow spike across an edge is the signal for an
evacuation or an incident, and the alert path is already built.

### Registration

`devices/`, not `simulators/`. A simulator is a fake you throw away; the collector *is* the
sensor, shipped to every building and running unattended. `edge/` would collide with the
ingress proxy, which is what "the edge" means everywhere else in this repository.

- `ap-collector: "devices/ap-collector"` in `.moon/workspace.yml`.
- An entry in `.github/services.json` with **no `image` key**. `ci-gate.yml` gates the
  docker matrix on `select(.image and (.ci != false))`, so omitting the image skips the
  build while the tests still run. Do **not** copy the simulators' `"ci": false` — that
  also drops the package from coverage and from `check-workflow-registry.py`, and
  union-by-MAC is the likeliest bug in the design.
- A job in `ci-gate.yml` using `tpl-python-ci.yml`, listed in both `docker.needs` and
  `ci-passed.needs` — `check-workflow-registry.py` fails otherwise.
- `moon.yml` overrides the inherited `test` / `lint`, which shell out to npm. Agent wraps
  uv in a `package.json`; this package cannot, because the root `.gitignore` ignores every
  `package.json`. Both overrides use `script:`, not `command:` — `command:` merges with the
  inherited task's args and silently runs `uv test`.
- Python, no runtime dependencies: ubus and `/telemetry/observations` are both JSON over HTTP,
  and signing is `hmac.new(key, body, sha256)`. `urllib.request` covers it.

---

## What Is Built, What Is Open

Built on branch `feat/collector-bursts`, against ap-simulator only:

| Part | Where |
|---|---|
| Burst grouping, MAC → token, one signed tick per poll (10 s) | `devices/ap-collector` |
| Tick contract | `twin_schema::WifiObservations`, `fixtures/wifi-observations.json` |
| Check, count per router room, republish to Kafka | telemetry `POST /telemetry/observations` |
| Estimator, tracks, accuracy test | digital-twin `estimator.rs`, `tracks.rs` |
| Dots to the browser | twin → Redis `twin:devices:{id}` → socket `devices` event → twin view, devices mode |
| Room counts by position | twin → Kafka `estimated-occupancy` → telemetry `estimatedOccupancy` |

Open, and only real hardware answers them:

- **A walk test on a real OpenWrt site.** The simulator's radio model and scan rates are guesses; the measured accuracy above holds only on them.
- **Probe addresses**: does a connected phone use one random address per burst (fine) or per frame (breaks grouping)? How often does it scan with the screen off?
- **Exit lag**: a router lists a departed phone until `ap_max_inactivity` (300 s). A time limit would fix it, but would also drop phones that are present but asleep.
- **Routers with no placement** add nothing to positions; a floor with none gets no dots and no `estimatedOccupancy`.

The risky unknowns are **physical, not code**: whether signals separate two rooms depends on
where the routers are mounted. Measure before tuning.

---

## Phase 2 — Dedicated Sniffers

Only once phase 1 runs in a real building. Adds coverage of devices that never associate.

Raspberry Pi with a dual-band monitor-mode NIC (mt7612u, ath9k), roughly €60 per unit. An
ESP32 is €5 but **2.4 GHz only**, and modern phones probe heavily on 5 GHz — systematic
undercount. Acceptable for a bench prototype, not for a building.

**Do not de-randomize.** The literature is good — `Bleach` reports ~99%, `vMac` and
`Espresso` combine information elements, sequence numbers and RSSI, one IE-attribute method
reports 99% precision across 70+ device types — and every one of them is re-identification
engineering. It buys accuracy this feature does not need, at the one cost it cannot pay.

**Count without identity instead.** `RateCount` (2025) estimates the device count from a
provably unbiased closed form over the *rate* at which probe frames arrive, with an error
model. Learning-free: no training set, no per-site model tuning, no MAC ever inspected. The
Keio scheme is the same idea — a phone emits a roughly constant probe rate, so the rate scales
with the population.

This is simultaneously the lazier engineering and the defensible privacy story.

Practical corrections the estimator needs: channel hopping means only a fraction of frames are
heard, so a duty-cycle term; probe rate varies by phone model and screen state.

---

## Deployment Prerequisites

Phase 1 is only correct if the Wi-Fi is configured for it. These are requirements, not
recommendations.

| Requirement | Why |
|---|---|
| **One SSID across all APs** | Per-SSID randomization means a second SSID is a second identity for the same phone. |
| **One SSID across 2.4 and 5 GHz** | Split-band SSIDs are two networks; use band steering. Standard practice regardless. |
| **WPA2-AES or WPA3** | On open networks Apple rotates the private address every 2 weeks. Encryption pins it. |
| **Every router placed in the twin** | A router sensor in its room, at its position in the twin editor, *is* the AP → room mapping and the estimator's geometry; the collector reads the list from `GET /telemetry/collector`. A router with no placement counts in `totalDeviceCount` but adds nothing to positions; the editor mounts a router given only a room 0.5 m below that room's ceiling centre. |
| **One read-only ubus login on the routers** | The collector's own file holds it (per-router `overrides` where one differs). Logins never reach the platform. |

---

## Known Error Sources

| Source | Effect | Handling |
|---|---|---|
| **Multiple devices per person** | Phone + laptop + watch = 3 counts, 1 human. Largest error term by far. | A stated conversion factor per building. Cannot be inferred. |
| **People with no device** | Undercount. | Inherent. Document the caveat. |
| **Visitors not on the Wi-Fi** | Undercount. | Phase 2. |
| **Boundary flapping** | A router-room count flips rooms each tick. | Twin's tracks smooth the dots and `estimatedOccupancy`; `totalDeviceCount` stays unsmoothed. |
| **Walls, antennas, shadowing** | Signals read weaker than the radio model expects; positions land a room off. | Grid fit with walls; measured 79.4% right room on simulated data. |
| **Ambiguous bursts** | A burst fitting two phones joins neither. | Dropped on purpose: no phone gets someone else's path. |
| **Roam leftovers** | Old AP lists a roamed device up to 300 s, signal frozen; holds it in its old room. | Least-idle row per MAC wins. Rows still tied, e.g. both unchanged since the collector started, fall back to strongest signal. |
| **Android re-randomize on reconnect** | Split identity. | Non-default per-network option. Rare; ignored. |

What is measured is **devices**; what a facility manager wants is **people**. That gap is the
conversion factor above, and it is why both numbers are published separately rather than one
being quietly substituted for the other. The caveat belongs in the user-facing docs too, not
only here: `ratioDeviceCount` and `estimatedOccupancy` are estimates and read like facts.

The conversion rounds **up**. One device at a factor of 2.5 is 0.4 of a person, and rounding
that to zero reports an occupied room as empty — the one fact the collector otherwise takes
care to keep distinct from a genuinely empty zone. An empty zone still converts to 0.

A rejected or unreachable tick POST is logged and dropped, not raised: the run loop calls
the post path directly, so an escaping error ends the collector on a single telemetry restart.
One lost batch costs one poll interval of resolution; the next tick supersedes it.

## Calibration

Every number that depends on the physical world is configuration, per building:

- devices-per-person conversion factor
- poll interval
- the estimator's radio model (1 m signal, path-loss exponent, wall loss); today ap-simulator's values
- phase 2: probes-per-device rate, channel-hop duty cycle

None of these are constants. A building full of engineers and a building full of visitors
have different device ratios, and no model can see that from the code.

Two numbers are *not* on that list, because they are ceilings rather than tuning:

- **`requestTimeoutS <= pollIntervalS`** (defaults 5 s and 10 s). Holds only because the
  collector polls every AP of a building concurrently. Polled in sequence a tick would cost the
  sum of its APs' timeouts, so enough dark APs would silently slow the real poll rate. One AP's own interfaces are still read in series, so an AP with many
  radios and a long timeout is the one case still worth checking by hand.

---

## Alternative — Consume the Vendor Controller

If the building runs Cisco, Aruba or Mist, the controller already computes client positions.
[Cisco Spaces Location Cloud API](https://developer.cisco.com/docs/cisco-spaces-location/)
returns cartesian `(x, y)` per floor; Mist is equivalent.

Zero hardware, zero algorithm — but vendor lock-in, and the vendor holds the MAC addresses.
That moves the privacy problem rather than removing it, and the platform then depends on a
commercial contract. Worth taking where the hardware already exists; not worth designing for.

---


## Prior Art & References

Ordered by how directly it feeds the build. The first group is code to read before writing
any; the research groups are there to justify — or overturn — a decision, not to be
reimplemented.

### Phase 1 — closest existing implementations

Read these first. Every one solves a piece of the collector.

- [vjt/openwrt-ha-presence](https://github.com/vjt/openwrt-ha-presence) — **the closest analogue.** Scrapes RSSI from every OpenWrt AP and assigns each device to a room by strongest signal. This is phase 1's algorithm, already written, at single-home scale.
- [awilliams/wifi-presence](https://github.com/awilliams/wifi-presence) — Go daemon on the AP itself, subscribing to hostapd's control interface for client connect/disconnect events rather than polling. Worth reading for the event-driven alternative to a poll loop.
- [WiFi Presence Detection for Home Assistant Using OpenWrt](https://sindro.me/posts/2026-02-15-wifi-presence-detection-home-assistant/) — end-to-end walkthrough. Enables the `wifi_stations` collector in `prometheus-node-exporter-lua`, then polls `/metrics` on each AP every 5 s for `wifi_station_signal_dbm`. The simplest transport for the collector: no ubus auth, no custom ACLs.
- [\[HOWTO\] Reliable WiFi presence for Home Assistant using OpenWrt hostapd events](https://community.home-assistant.io/t/howto-reliable-wifi-presence-for-home-assistant-using-openwrt-hostapd-events/980611) — the practical failure modes of naive presence detection. Read it for what goes wrong, not for the architecture.
- [FUjr/homeassistant-openwrt-ubus](https://github.com/FUjr/homeassistant-openwrt-ubus) — working `ubus` client pulling per-station RSSI, connection quality and which AP interface each device is on.

### Phase 1 — the ubus / hostapd surface

- [OpenWrt hostapd `ubus` interface (source)](https://github.com/openwrt/openwrt/blob/master/package/network/services/hostapd/src/src/ap/ubus.c) — authoritative definition of `get_clients` and the rest. The docs lag; the source does not.
- [ubus (OpenWrt micro bus architecture) — techref](https://oldwiki.archive.openwrt.org/doc/techref/ubus) — the JSON-RPC-over-HTTP protocol: `session.login` for a token, then `call` with the object and method.
- [OnionIoT — ubus tutorial (part 3 covers rpcd)](https://github.com/OnionIoT/wiki/tree/master/Tutorials/OpenWrt%20Tutorials/UBUS_Tutorial) — the clearest walkthrough of exposing and calling ubus methods remotely.
- [Easy ubus daemons with rpcd (Freifunk)](https://blog.freifunk.net/2017/07/02/easy-ubus-daemons-rpcd/) and [ubus service development with rpcd (Zilogic)](https://zilogic.com/blog/ubus-service-development-with-rpcd.html) — needed if the collector ends up running *on* the AP.
- [openwrt-ubus-rpc (PyPI)](https://pypi.org/project/openwrt-ubus-rpc/) — Python client that already wraps login and `get_hostapd_clients`.
- [OpenWrt forum — UBUS over HTTP for WiFi presence detection](https://forum.archive.openwrt.org/viewtopic.php?id=65004) — the ACL file under `/usr/share/rpcd/acl.d/` needed to grant hostapd methods to a non-root RPC user. Easy to miss; nothing works without it.
- [Home Assistant — OpenWrt (ubus) integration](https://www.home-assistant.io/integrations/ubus/) — documents the exact router-side setup (`rpcd`, `uhttpd-mod-ubus`, ACL) in user-facing terms.

### Trilateration — reference implementations

Circles lost to the grid fit on simulated data (69% vs 95% right room); listed so the comparison is checkable.

- [ifindev/indoor-positioning-algorithms](https://github.com/ifindev/indoor-positioning-algorithms) — trilateration and min-max in Python over RSSI from four routers.
- [dvalenza/Trilateration-Example](https://github.com/dvalenza/Trilateration-Example) — minimal three-signal position solve.
- [WiFi Trilateration With Three or More Points](https://www.appelsiini.net/2017/trilateration-with-n-points/) — the least-squares generalisation, and the path-loss model `d = 10 ^ ((Ptx - RSSI) / (10n))` whose exponent `n` is the per-site calibration that makes this expensive.
- [Indoor Positioning and Tracking in a Multi-Level Residential Building Using WiFi (MDPI)](https://www.mdpi.com/2078-2489/16/8/633) — accuracy actually achieved in a real multi-floor building.

### Phase 2 — sniffers and probe capture

- [schollz/howmanypeoplearearound](https://github.com/schollz/howmanypeoplearearound) — counts probe requests over a window via `tshark` on a monitor-mode adapter. Small, readable, and the direct ancestor of the rate-based approach.
- [GiovanniBaccichet/openwrt-multi-sniffer](https://github.com/GiovanniBaccichet/openwrt-multi-sniffer) — an OpenWrt AP sniffing probe requests across multiple channels in **both** 2.4 and 5 GHz. Bridges phase 1 and phase 2 hardware: it may remove the need for separate Pi units entirely.
- [fdd4s/wifi-analytics](https://github.com/fdd4s/wifi-analytics) — captures MAC and RSSI from monitor mode; a worked presence-analytics pipeline.
- [ETS-PoliTO/esp32-sniffer](https://github.com/ETS-PoliTO/esp32-sniffer) — reference ESP32 firmware for probe-request capture.
- [SensorsIot/Wi-Fi-Sniffer-as-a-Human-detector](https://github.com/SensorsIot/Wi-Fi-Sniffer-as-a-Human-detector) — companion code to the video below.
- [Building an ESP32 packet sniffer (HackMag)](https://hackmag.com/security/esp32-sniffer-2) — monitor-mode walkthrough, and the 2.4 GHz-only limitation stated plainly.
- [Kismet, monitor mode, wardriving on OpenWrt (forum thread)](https://forum.openwrt.org/t/kismet-monitor-mode-wardriving/167431) — Kismet is fully passive and handles channel hopping, logging and device tracking already. Viable as the capture layer under a custom estimator.

### Phase 3 — device-free CSI sensing

Not planned. Tracked because it removes the identifier problem entirely.

- [espressif/esp-csi](https://github.com/espressif/esp-csi) — official CSI support, including a `wifi_sensing_demo` for motion and human presence with on-site training.
- [ESP32 CSI Toolkit](https://stevenmhernandez.github.io/ESP32-CSI-Tool/) ([repo](https://github.com/StevenMHernandez/ESP32-CSI-Tool)) — extracts CSI from an ESP32 in active or passive mode.
- [Wi-ESP CSI Tool](https://wrlab.github.io/Wi-ESP/) — alternative toolkit.
- [Awesome-WiFi-CSI-Sensing](https://github.com/NTUMARS/Awesome-WiFi-CSI-Sensing) — curated paper and tooling index. The single best entry point to this field.

### Standards and platform behaviour

- [Apple — Use private Wi-Fi addresses](https://support.apple.com/en-us/102509) — fixed vs. rotating, and which networks rotate. The source for the WPA2/WPA3 prerequisite.
- [Android — MAC randomization behavior (AOSP)](https://source.android.com/docs/core/connect/wifi-mac-randomization-behavior) — per-profile persistence rules.
- [Android — Implement MAC randomization (AOSP)](https://source.android.com/docs/core/connect/wifi-mac-randomization) — derivation of the address from the network profile.
- [IEEE 802.11bf: Enabling the Widespread Adoption of Wi-Fi Sensing (NIST)](https://www.nist.gov/publications/ieee-80211bf-enabling-widespread-adoption-wi-fi-sensing) — device-free sensing; active standard since September 2025, chipsets still thin.
- [Indoor Positioning with Wi-Fi Location: a survey of 802.11mc/az/bk FTM (arXiv 2509.03901)](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2509.03901) — accuracy per amendment, and why FTM is client-initiated.

### Counting and localization research

- [RateCount: Learning-Free Device Counting by Wi-Fi Probe Listening (arXiv 2507.03873)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.03873) — the phase 2 estimator. Unbiased closed form over probe arrival rate; no training, no MAC inspected.
- [MAC address randomization tolerant crowd monitoring (arXiv 2112.02161)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2112.02161) — Vision and TrueSight; also uses data and beacon frames, not probes alone.
- [Indoor crowd estimation using the number of Wi-Fi probe requests (Keio)](https://keio.elsevierpure.com/en/publications/indoor-crowd-estimation-scheme-using-the-number-of-wi-fi-probe-re/) — the constant-probe-rate assumption RateCount formalises.
- [Signals from the Crowd: Uncovering Social Relationships (IMC 2013)](https://conferences.sigcomm.org/imc/2013/papers/imc148-barberaSP106.pdf) — the origin of large-scale probe analytics, and a blunt demonstration of what this data reveals when it is *not* aggregated. Read it as a privacy argument.
- [MAC Address Anonymization for Crowd Counting (arXiv 2009.09876)](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2009.09876) — why salted hashing is not anonymisation, with collision-rate requirements.
- [MAC address de-randomization for WiFi device counting (ScienceDirect)](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1389128622004273) — the approach deliberately rejected.
- [Efficient Association of Wi-Fi Probe Requests under MAC Randomization — vMac (INFOCOM'21)](https://www.cse.ust.hk/~gchan/papers/info21_vMac.pdf) — likewise: state of the art in the direction not taken.

### Datasets and simulation

For validating an estimator before any hardware exists.

- [CRAWDAD `sapienza/probe-requests` (IEEE DataPort)](https://ieee-dataport.org/open-access/crawdad-sapienzaprobe-requests) — the classic set: four one-shot events of 40 min to 6 h, plus six weeks of fixed capture on a campus.
- [Labelled device Wi-Fi probe requests (Mendeley, 2024)](https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/5tvnwhsj2p/2) — 22 devices in 6 modes each. The only public set where the emitting device is labelled, so it is the only one that can score an estimator's error.
- [Dataset of Wi-Fi Probe Requests (arXiv 2308.04435)](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2308.04435)
- [WiFiSim: simulating Wi-Fi probe requests via AOSP analysis (arXiv 2509.15501)](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2509.15501) — generate synthetic probe traffic with realistic device behaviour. Useful for testing before a Pi exists, in the same spirit as `simulators/`.

### Videos

Listed by their published title; orientation, not specification.

- [#163 Wi-Fi Sniffer as Sensor for Humans](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fmhjtzmLrg8) — end-to-end build of a probe-request human detector, with the companion repo above.
- [Probe Requests: What They Do And How To Save Them](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pycdTNSWbaw) — what a probe request contains and how capture works.

### Privacy and law

- [Autoriteit Persoonsgegevens — WiFi and bluetooth tracking](https://www.autoriteitpersoonsgegevens.nl/en/themes/internet-and-smart-devices/wifi-and-bluetooth) — MACs as indirectly identifying data; conditions for anonymisation; the multiple-location-points rule this design is built around.
- [AP fines municipality for Wi-Fi tracking (2021)](https://www.autoriteitpersoonsgegevens.nl/en/current/ap-fines-municipality-for-wi-fi-tracking) — enforcement precedent.
- [WiFi-Tracking and Retail Analytics under the GDPR (TechGDPR)](https://techgdpr.com/blog/wifi-tracking-retail-analytics-gdpr/) — legal-basis analysis for exactly this class of system.
- [What does the GDPR say about WiFi tracking? (Privacy Company)](https://www.privacycompany.eu/blog/what-does-the-gdpr-say-about-wifi-tracking) — why consent is not a workable basis for ambient sensing.

### Vendor controller APIs

If the building already runs managed Wi-Fi, the position is computed for you.

- [Cisco Spaces Location Cloud API](https://developer.cisco.com/docs/cisco-spaces-location/) — active client locations, history, map and AP endpoints; cartesian `(x, y)` per floor.
- [Cisco Spaces — access point auto-location](https://www.cisco.com/c/en/us/td/docs/wireless/spaces/config-guide/ciscospaces-configuration-guide/m-device-placement.html) — how AP placement is derived; here the twin editor's router placement plays that role.
- [Juniper Mist — adding an AP for location](https://www.mist.com/documentation/adding-ap-location/) — note the requirement of line of sight to at least two APs.
